1
00:00:01,333 --> 00:00:04,510
In 2015, a Nobel Prize recognised the discovery of

2
00:00:04,510 --> 00:00:07,333
artemisinin, a drug against malaria.

3
00:00:08,467 --> 00:00:10,800
Its discoverer found the clue in a Chinese medical

4
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:13,267
handbook about sixteen hundred years old.

5
00:00:14,367 --> 00:00:16,123
Europe has a handbook like that too.

6
00:00:16,123 --> 00:00:17,800
And it is even older.

7
00:00:33,263 --> 00:00:35,483
Hi, I'm Arpit.

8
00:00:35,563 --> 00:00:38,423
Currently I'm pursuing B.Tech in Computer Science from National

9
00:00:38,423 --> 00:00:40,603
Institute of Technology, Hamirpur,

10
00:00:40,603 --> 00:00:45,343
in India. Before I show you what we built,

11
00:00:45,403 --> 00:00:47,643
let me introduce someone it's for.

12
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:53,119
Meet Eleni, a pharmacognosy PhD student in Athens.

13
00:00:53,119 --> 00:00:56,035
Her lab can afford to screen ten plant extracts

14
00:00:56,035 --> 00:00:57,300
this year.

15
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:01,950
Her supervisor hands her Dioscorides:

16
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:05,065
De materia medica, written in the first century,

17
00:01:05,065 --> 00:01:08,017
and copied by hand for fifteen hundred years.

18
00:01:08,017 --> 00:01:10,367
Somewhere in its six hundred plants,

19
00:01:10,367 --> 00:01:12,333
he says, is a lead.

20
00:01:14,667 --> 00:01:16,475
The first problem is the names.

21
00:01:16,475 --> 00:01:19,902
Take asphodel. The standard Greek dictionary says one species.

22
00:01:19,902 --> 00:01:22,520
The experts who studied this text say another.

23
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:24,833
A third is just as plausible.

24
00:01:27,167 --> 00:01:29,875
Pick the wrong plant, and every experiment after that

25
00:01:29,875 --> 00:01:31,033
is wasted.

26
00:01:33,367 --> 00:01:34,427
The second problem:

27
00:01:34,427 --> 00:01:36,224
which of these remedies survived?

28
00:01:36,224 --> 00:01:37,145
Did Galen agree?

29
00:01:37,145 --> 00:01:38,344
Did Byzantine doctors?

30
00:01:38,344 --> 00:01:40,187
Do Greek villages still use them?

31
00:01:40,187 --> 00:01:42,169
Until now, that was traced by hand,

32
00:01:42,169 --> 00:01:43,967
one plant at a time.

33
00:01:46,267 --> 00:01:47,142
And the third:

34
00:01:47,142 --> 00:01:50,450
does ancient use actually predict what modern science goes

35
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:51,229
on to study?

36
00:01:51,229 --> 00:01:54,067
Almost nobody has tested that honestly.

37
00:01:57,167 --> 00:01:59,207
Why does this matter?

38
00:01:59,207 --> 00:02:02,607
Plants have given medicine some of the most important

39
00:02:02,607 --> 00:02:05,347
drugs, and the Greek tradition is one of the

40
00:02:05,347 --> 00:02:07,267
best documented anywhere.

41
00:02:07,647 --> 00:02:11,507
Yet almost none of it ever became a hypothesis anyone

42
00:02:11,507 --> 00:02:13,547
can actually test.

43
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:19,900
So we built Pharmakon.

44
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:22,595
It reads the Greek, works out which plant each

45
00:02:22,595 --> 00:02:24,809
name means, with an honest probability,

46
00:02:24,809 --> 00:02:27,360
follows every use through two thousand years,

47
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:29,478
tests the idea against later science,

48
00:02:29,478 --> 00:02:32,100
and hands Eleni a ranked shortlist.

49
00:02:36,433 --> 00:02:38,972
It starts from one unbroken chain of texts.

50
00:02:38,972 --> 00:02:40,876
Dioscorides in the first century:

51
00:02:40,876 --> 00:02:42,292
fifteen hundred passages.

52
00:02:42,292 --> 00:02:43,513
Galen in the second:

53
00:02:43,513 --> 00:02:45,954
four hundred and seventy six drug entries.

54
00:02:45,954 --> 00:02:48,102
John the Physician in the thirteenth,

55
00:02:48,102 --> 00:02:50,787
where experts identified sixty one plant names.

56
00:02:50,787 --> 00:02:54,009
And more than twelve hundred records from Greek villages

57
00:02:54,009 --> 00:02:55,000
today.

58
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:59,200
Around it, eight open databases:

59
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:02,464
where each plant grows, ten thousand plant compounds,

60
00:03:02,464 --> 00:03:05,780
human proteins, diseases, research papers and toxicity.

61
00:03:05,780 --> 00:03:07,412
Every source checked live.

62
00:03:07,412 --> 00:03:09,433
Every licence recorded.

63
00:03:13,167 --> 00:03:14,477
First, Pharmakon reads.

64
00:03:14,477 --> 00:03:17,195
A Greek lexicon and a language model both mark

65
00:03:17,195 --> 00:03:20,058
every medical use, and every claim must quote the

66
00:03:20,058 --> 00:03:21,950
exact Greek words that state it.

67
00:03:21,950 --> 00:03:24,037
If the quote is not in the source,

68
00:03:24,037 --> 00:03:26,464
character for character, it is thrown out.

69
00:03:26,464 --> 00:03:30,267
All five thousand claims from the language model passed.

70
00:03:32,167 --> 00:03:33,569
Second, it identifies.

71
00:03:33,569 --> 00:03:36,480
For each ancient name it weighs the evidence.

72
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:38,475
Does the plant grow in Greece?

73
00:03:38,475 --> 00:03:41,602
Does its modern Greek name echo the ancient one?

74
00:03:41,602 --> 00:03:42,789
Do scholars agree?

75
00:03:42,789 --> 00:03:45,161
And does Dioscorides' own description,

76
00:03:45,161 --> 00:03:47,426
the leaves, the flowers, the roots,

77
00:03:47,426 --> 00:03:48,720
match a modern one?

78
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:51,955
The answer is a probability, with an honest option

79
00:03:51,955 --> 00:03:53,633
for unidentified.

80
00:03:55,567 --> 00:03:57,966
Third, it traces every use through Galen,

81
00:03:57,966 --> 00:04:00,219
John the Physician and modern villages,

82
00:04:00,219 --> 00:04:02,716
and compares the result with shuffled data,

83
00:04:02,716 --> 00:04:05,667
so coincidence cannot pass for tradition.

84
00:04:07,567 --> 00:04:08,955
Fourth, the time machine.

85
00:04:08,955 --> 00:04:10,714
Stand in the year two thousand,

86
00:04:10,714 --> 00:04:12,334
or five, or ten, or fifteen.

87
00:04:12,334 --> 00:04:14,000
Use only what was known then.

88
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,870
Rank the plant and disease pairs nobody had studied.

89
00:04:16,870 --> 00:04:18,906
Then check what science studied next.

90
00:04:18,906 --> 00:04:21,776
No language model output is allowed anywhere in this

91
00:04:21,776 --> 00:04:22,700
test.

92
00:04:25,357 --> 00:04:27,137
Let me show you.

93
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:30,667
Here is Eleni using it.

94
00:04:33,967 --> 00:04:35,692
She searches for apsinthion.

95
00:04:35,692 --> 00:04:38,463
Wormwood. Pharmakon shows Dioscorides' passage,

96
00:04:38,463 --> 00:04:41,833
with every use highlighted in his own words.

97
00:04:45,167 --> 00:04:46,243
Which plant is it?

98
00:04:46,243 --> 00:04:48,724
Artemisia absinthium, at seventy five percent,

99
00:04:48,724 --> 00:04:50,878
with the uncertainty drawn as a range,

100
00:04:50,878 --> 00:04:54,333
and twenty three percent that it is something else.

101
00:04:57,633 --> 00:04:58,632
She can see why.

102
00:04:58,632 --> 00:05:01,249
It grows in Greece, the Latin name echoes the

103
00:05:01,249 --> 00:05:03,800
Greek, and the description matches.

104
00:05:07,133 --> 00:05:08,381
Then the long view:

105
00:05:08,381 --> 00:05:10,721
every use, from Dioscorides, to Galen,

106
00:05:10,721 --> 00:05:14,233
to John the Physician, to Greek villages today.

107
00:05:17,567 --> 00:05:18,379
Now the leads.

108
00:05:18,379 --> 00:05:20,681
She asks for plants we are at least fifty

109
00:05:20,681 --> 00:05:23,615
percent sure of, hides every plant with known toxicity,

110
00:05:23,615 --> 00:05:25,433
and picks a condition.

111
00:05:28,767 --> 00:05:31,007
Each card shows how sure we are of the

112
00:05:31,007 --> 00:05:33,200
plant, and whether a full chain exists,

113
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:35,580
from a plant compound, to a human protein,

114
00:05:35,580 --> 00:05:37,067
to the disease.

115
00:05:40,367 --> 00:05:41,167
She opens one.

116
00:05:41,167 --> 00:05:43,389
There is no pharmacology paper on it yet.

117
00:05:43,389 --> 00:05:45,744
Here is the exact search, and it comes back

118
00:05:45,744 --> 00:05:49,100
empty. But the chemistry already points at a target.

119
00:05:52,433 --> 00:05:54,267
She downloads the shortlist.

120
00:05:57,567 --> 00:06:00,115
And she does not even need our website.

121
00:06:00,115 --> 00:06:02,185
Pharmakon is also an MCP server.

122
00:06:02,185 --> 00:06:03,936
In Claude, she simply asks:

123
00:06:03,936 --> 00:06:07,333
which plants did Dioscorides use for eye diseases that

124
00:06:07,333 --> 00:06:09,367
nobody has tested yet?

125
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:14,322
Claude calls Pharmakon's tools,

126
00:06:14,322 --> 00:06:16,168
and answers with the probabilities,

127
00:06:16,168 --> 00:06:18,105
the toxicity flags and the evidence,

128
00:06:18,105 --> 00:06:19,967
straight from our data.

129
00:06:23,267 --> 00:06:26,249
Developers get the same data through an open API,

130
00:06:26,249 --> 00:06:30,033
and anyone can rebuild every number with one command.

131
00:06:33,267 --> 00:06:34,717
Underneath is a knowledge graph:

132
00:06:34,717 --> 00:06:36,168
two thousand five hundred nodes,

133
00:06:36,168 --> 00:06:38,333
and more than nine thousand links.

134
00:06:39,633 --> 00:06:41,466
A lead is a path through it:

135
00:06:41,466 --> 00:06:44,062
from the Greek words, to the ancient name,

136
00:06:44,062 --> 00:06:45,945
to the species, to a compound,

137
00:06:45,945 --> 00:06:48,083
to a human target, to the disease.

138
00:06:48,083 --> 00:06:49,916
Every step carries its source.

139
00:06:49,916 --> 00:06:52,664
So every lead traces back to the exact words

140
00:06:52,664 --> 00:06:54,333
Dioscorides wrote.

141
00:06:58,567 --> 00:07:00,200
So, does it work?

142
00:07:01,733 --> 00:07:04,477
On the sixty one names that experts have identified,

143
00:07:04,477 --> 00:07:07,177
Pharmakon agrees with them sixty two percent of the

144
00:07:07,177 --> 00:07:10,300
time. The standard dictionary manages thirty one.

145
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:14,049
Its probabilities are getting more honest.

146
00:07:14,049 --> 00:07:17,170
Comparing descriptions cut the calibration error from point

147
00:07:17,170 --> 00:07:18,684
two one to point one four.

148
00:07:18,684 --> 00:07:20,061
Our target is point one.

149
00:07:20,061 --> 00:07:21,900
We are not there yet.

150
00:07:23,433 --> 00:07:25,028
The uses really did survive.

151
00:07:25,028 --> 00:07:27,749
Forty nine percent recur in Greek villages today,

152
00:07:27,749 --> 00:07:30,367
against forty one percent by chance.

153
00:07:32,063 --> 00:07:35,603
Now, the result we didn't expect.

154
00:07:35,703 --> 00:07:40,123
We asked, does 2,000 years of history predict what

155
00:07:40,123 --> 00:07:42,283
science studies next?

156
00:07:42,303 --> 00:07:45,443
It doesn't. Research popularity does.

157
00:07:45,443 --> 00:07:50,763
And in 2005, adding history even made the ranking

158
00:07:50,763 --> 00:07:54,503
worse. We report that exactly as we would report

159
00:07:54,503 --> 00:07:55,503
a success.

160
00:07:57,167 --> 00:08:00,319
For Eleni, that means one thousand seven hundred and

161
00:08:00,319 --> 00:08:01,895
eighty one untested leads.

162
00:08:01,895 --> 00:08:04,743
Sixteen of the top twenty have a full chemical

163
00:08:04,743 --> 00:08:06,980
path. Seven carry a toxicity warning,

164
00:08:06,980 --> 00:08:08,800
shown, never hidden.

165
00:08:11,533 --> 00:08:13,854
For labs, a shortlist with reasons.

166
00:08:13,854 --> 00:08:16,839
For historians, Dioscorides read with evidence.

167
00:08:16,839 --> 00:08:20,533
For conservation, every species with its status.

168
00:08:21,830 --> 00:08:24,450
We are clear about the limits.

169
00:08:24,610 --> 00:08:27,350
Expert judgement is not ground truth.

170
00:08:27,790 --> 00:08:30,870
A search hit is only a proxy for evidence,

171
00:08:30,870 --> 00:08:35,030
and our audits still need human experts.

172
00:08:35,830 --> 00:08:38,570
Everything here is a research hypothesis,

173
00:08:38,570 --> 00:08:40,330
never medical advice.

174
00:08:40,407 --> 00:08:43,627
Next: expert audits, richer plant descriptions,

175
00:08:43,627 --> 00:08:47,007
more Byzantine authors, and a lab partner to test

176
00:08:47,007 --> 00:08:50,187
our top leads.

177
00:08:50,347 --> 00:08:54,487
If that's you, we'd love to hear from you.

178
00:08:57,533 --> 00:08:59,608
Eleni started with six hundred names.

179
00:08:59,608 --> 00:09:01,965
She ends with a shortlist she can defend,

180
00:09:01,965 --> 00:09:03,367
word by word.

181
00:09:04,667 --> 00:09:05,967
Pharmakon.
